Qt Overview
- cross platform framework
- can be used to build your own UI for apps
- great for for UI
    - not only for UI, helpful for file handling, networking, etc.
- widely adopted by VFX industry, many DCCs are built using Qt
- far more control and flexibility than maya native UI
- learning curve can be steep, some unique design patterns
- Qt version updates can potentially cause breaks with programs that use them
    - the is a necessary evil for a central framework

Qt Versions in Maya
- Starting in Maya 2025 Qt6(Pyside6) was adopted, prior to this is was Qt5 (PySide2)
    - most of the breaking changes are in complicated ares of the code

Qt Windows
- QtCore module holds many useful functionality and should usually be imported along with QtWidgets
- QDialog good for short lived UIs
- the QtWidget base class has so many useful functions and variables to achieve most UI functionality you could desire
- By default in window, when a window gains focus it becomes the top level widget and any other widgets fall behind
    - the exception is windows that are children of the top level window
    - must parent our widgets to Maya so they do not fall behind
- no direct method to get mayas main window
- widgets can act as windows
- widgets can parent to any other widget
- QWidget is the base class of all widgets
- you should have an OOP mindset when building UI
    - break it down into as many different widgets as required
    - do not try to create one monolithic widget
- layouts are not widgets
- layouts are imperative for organizing widgets cleanly
- generally widgets will be parented to a layout which is then parented to a widget or other layout
- because layouts can be added or parent to other layouts you can create complicated nested layouts
- widget and layout creation should be encapsulated within functions
- during tool development it is important to create the behaviour where when tool is opened, any previous instances are
destroyed and a new instance is created so that we can test our changes
- but in production what should occur is that the first time the tool is opened, and instance is created, and then when
the tool is closed it does not actually close, but rather sets its UI to hidden
    - benefit to this is that the tool will have memory inherently


Signal and Slots
- allow for communication between two objects
    - command pattern
- provides interactivity for UI
- signal is the action that occurred (button clicked)
- slot is the method that is called when signal occurs
- connection is the link between a signal and a slot
- Qt documentation, each widget has a signals section showing what signals are available
    - auto complete does not work great for signals
- Qt documentation recommends that you decorate all methods used as slots with @QtCore.Slot()
    - ensuring you match args
    - slot method def slot_method(arg_1), then decorator looks like @QtCore.Slot(arg_1)
    - reason for this is that it can cause unnecessary overhead or might cause issues with QML
    - due to python not supporting method overloading, the decorator helps distinguish methods
    - most use cases are for backwards compatibility, need to keep in the back of mind
- slots (methods) can safely ignore too many arguments being passed to them from signals but not the other way around
    - slots should use the *args
    - slots should query relevant info in the method and not expect info to be passed in
- there are many helpful signals already built into most widgets but you can create your own
- to add new signals to a class you must subclass

