Widgets, Actions, Menus
- Qt has a wide range of pre built widgets available to use
- don't reinvent the wheel, just do the research on each widget for full potential and ease of development
- *charcoal editor was made with QtWidgets
- stack widget = index list on side, each index acts as tab, good for settings/options
- combo box = drop down list, can store generic data with each option
- spin box = numerical values only, but editable and scrollable
- group box = nice title and shaded box, can contain any types of widgets, good polish
- button groups = not a widget, does not change appearance, manages state of buttons
    - good for grouping buttons and creating the radio button effect (for none radio buttons)
    - if you have many radio buttons but they are not all connected, might need to use this separate them
- text edit/plain text edit
    - much more complex as they imitates things like notepad
    - most useful for read only output windows
    - NOTE if using for read only, you have to handle key presses or they get passed up the parent chain
- tab widget = like windows properties menus
    - ideally you want to create subclasses of QWidget for each tab you have
        - can we not add layouts?
- key presses in Qt get passed up the parent chain if the widget does not handle them
    - def keyPressEvent(): Super()
- because the maya main window will likely always be the final parent, it will receive the unhandled key presses and
likely perform unintended actions which is undesirable and confusing
- prevent by overriding keyPressEvent() and not calling Super()
    - or calling Super() for specific keys
- list view vs list widget
    - list view has more features but more overhead
    - can have multiple items
    - can have different selection modes allowing just single/multiple item selection
- stack widget
    - does not act like stacks in programming
    - similar to tab widget in that only one of the widgets is visible except there is not tabs
    - changing what widget is visible needs to be run by code
- menu bars
    - allow for easy creation of default menus
    - uses QActions to drive functionality
    - layouts contain special set method for menus (layout.setMenuBar)
        - to ensure typical spacing and look
- QAction
    - abstraction for user commands which can be added to different UI components
        - command pattern
        - in maya there are many buttons to achieve the same action
            - the UI might be different but they all reference the same action instance
    - can add shortcuts to action through action.setShortcut(QtGui.QKeySequence("Ctrl+Shift+A"))
        - this ensures that key presses from shortcuts get consumed by the widget
- menus can be nested, they will act as branches in the tree, actions are the leafs (final node) of the menus
- table widget
    - item based table view
    - basically a spread sheet like excel
    - view, store and manipulate data
    





